    {
      "path": "BBDD/Custom/Casalino/README.txt",
      "text": "LAST MODIFICATION:\t\tDate: 24/04/2026\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\r\nDATA SOURCES:\t\t\tMeasurements at the wind tunnel of the CMT-Clean Mobility & Thermofluid institute\r\n\t\t\t\tNumerical simulations carried out at the CMT-Clean Mobility & Thermofluid institute\r\n\t\t\t\tThe geometry was reconstructed using the data provided in https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ast.2021.106707.\r\n\t\t\t\tTwo more versions of the propeller increasing/decreasing the collective pitch by 5 degrees are manufactured and experimentally measured.\r\n\r\nNUMERICAL DATA:\r\n---------------\r\nStarCCM -> Results obtained using Star-CCM+\r\n\r\nurans\t-> Results from unsteady RANS using Sliding Mesh\r\n\r\nSimulations were carried out with the flow at different angles (measured with respect to the axis of rotation). 0 degrees mean pure axial flight.\r\n\r\nEXPERIMENTAL DATA:\r\n------------------\r\nexp\t\t\t\t\t-> Measurements of the propeller printed in ASA and SLA (Grey V4) at hover and different wind velocities"
    },
    {
      "path": "BBDD/README.txt",
      "text": "This dataset contains experimental measurements and numerical results of different propeller geometrie. The dataset is developed in the context of project ENOLA. \r\n\r\nThere is a \"GeneralInfo\" Excel file listing the propellers for which data is available, along with the specific data (experimental measurements, numerical results, simulations, etc.) provided for each one.\r\n\r\nThe propellers are grouped in \"Families\" of related propellers. Inside each propeller folder a README explaining the existing data can be found. Data is divided in two categories:\r\n\t- results: here experimental measurements and results from numerical simulations are provided. The possible results may include forces, moments and acoustic emissions at different conditions.\r\n\t- geometry: here geometry data of the propeller is provided when available. This may include CAD geometry, twist and chord distribution, airfoils used, etc.\r\n\r\nDisclaimer\r\n\r\nThis dataset is provided on a best-effort basis for research and educational purposes only. While reasonable care has been taken in its preparation, the authors make no representations or warranties of any kind, express or implied, regarding the accuracy, completeness, reliability, or suitability of the data. The dataset must not be used for operational, safety-critical, navigational, or decision-making purposes. The authors and affiliated institutions accept no liability for any loss, damage, or consequences arising directly or indirectly from the use of this dataset. Users assume full responsibility for any application of the data beyond its intended scope."
    },
    {
      "path": "BBDD/T-Motor/MS1101/README.txt",
      "text": "LAST MODIFICATION:\t\tDate: 02/09/2025\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\tPerson: Federico N. Ram\u00edrez\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\r\nDATA SOURCES:\t\t\tMeasurements at the wind tunnel of the CMT-Clean Mobility & Thermofluid institute\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\tNumerical simulations carried out at the CMT-Clean Mobility & Thermofluid institute\r\n\r\nNOTES:\t\t\t\t\tThree different softwares where evaluated (Star-CCM+, Fluent and OpenFOAM)\r\n\r\nNUMERICAL DATA:\r\n---------------\r\nStarCCM -> Results obtained using Star-CCM+\r\nFluent\t-> Results obtained using Ansys Fluent\r\nOF\t\t-> Results obtained using OpenFOAM\r\n\r\nvbm\t\t-> Results from steady RANS using Virtual Blade Model\r\nmrf\t\t-> Results from steady RANS using Moving Reference Frame\r\nurans\t-> Results from unsteady RANS using Sliding Mesh\r\ndes\t\t-> Results from Detached Eddy Simulation using Sliding Mesh\r\n\r\nEXPERIMENTAL DATA:\r\n------------------\r\nexp\t\t-> Measurements of the original COTS propeller. Measurements were produced with the flow at different angles (measured with respect to the axis of rotation). 0 degrees mean pure axial flight.\r\n\r\nACOUSTICS:\r\n----------\r\nInside the results folder.\r\nContains acoustic experimental measurements at 5 different rotational velocities (at hover).\r\nThe microphones are located at a 2 m. radius and equispaced 30 degrees. \r\nMic1 is 60 degrees below the plane of rotation. Mic2 is 30 degrees below the plane of rotation. Mic3 is at the plane of rotation. Mic4 is 30 degrees above the plane of rotation. Mic5 is 60 degrees above the plane of rotation. Mic 6 is 90 degrees above the plane of rotation (upstreams, at the axis of rotation).\r\n"
    },
